{"step_index":1816,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-27T06:46:34Z","input_tokens":3176,"cache_read_tokens":28452,"output_tokens":1688,"thinking":"Considering the navigation design, a prominent top bar switcher seems optimal for clear distinction between the \"Code Visualizer\" and the \"DSA Learning\" dashboard. The switcher should provide immediate access to both the existing coding workspace and the new data structures and algorithms learning environment. This setup allows for distinct user flows and a clean separation of concerns.\n\n\nThe \"DSA Learning\" view necessitates hiding the existing visualizer's specific elements and controls. A dedicated container, alongside a redesigned top bar with DSA-specific actions, becomes the primary focus. The visualizer's code area is dynamically populated with problem code when a user selects a problem. Practice progress is saved via local storage.\n\n\nThe system will now track problem attempts and solutions, updating local storage accordingly. On solution or completion of visualization, the problem's ID is recorded as `solved`. The learning dashboard will display overall progress, including total problems, solved, attempted, and not started.\n\nProgress breakdown will include topic-wise and difficulty-wise analysis, with visual representations of solved problems within each category. The filtering will be enabled by a topic selection.\n\n\nA \"Continue Learning\" card at the top will either show the most recently practiced problem or recommend the first problem if the user hasn't started. The system stores recently practiced problems in local storage, including title, topic, pattern, difficulty, status, and last practice time with a visualization button. A beginner-friendly roadmap will be implemented as a structured learning path with eight distinct stages.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\CVT\\\\.gemini\\\\antigravity\\\\scratch\\\\code-flow-visualizer\\\\app_build\\\\app.js\"","EndLine":"80","StartLine":"1","toolAction":"\"Inspecting app.js architecture\"","toolSummary":"\"View app.js header and run logic\""}}]}
